<article class="post post-with-toc post-toc-start">
<header class="post-header">
<h1>What a sitemap does, and what it does not</h1>
<div class="post-meta">
<address>
<ul class="people" role="list" aria-label="Written by">
<li class="person"><span><a rel="author" href="/blog/authors/lena-vogt/">Lena Vogt</a><span class="person-role">Technical SEO editor</span></span></li>
</ul>
</address>
<p>Published <time datetime="2026-09-28">28 September 2026</time></p>
<p class="reading-time"><time datetime="PT1M">1 min read</time></p>
<p>Filed under <a href="/blog/category/technical-seo/">Technical SEO</a></p>
</div>
</header>
<details class="toc-inline">
<summary>Contents</summary>
<nav aria-label="Contents">
<ol>
<li><a href="#what-it-does">What it does</a></li>
<li><a href="#what-it-does-not-do">What it does not do</a></li>
<li><a href="#keep-it-clean">Keep it clean</a></li>
</ol>
</nav>
</details>
<nav class="toc-side" aria-label="Contents">
<p class="toc-title">Contents</p>
<ol>
<li><a href="#what-it-does">What it does</a></li>
<li><a href="#what-it-does-not-do">What it does not do</a></li>
<li><a href="#keep-it-clean">Keep it clean</a></li>
</ol>
</nav>
<div class="post-body">
<p>A sitemap is a file, usually at <code>/sitemap.xml</code>, that lists the addresses of the pages on your site. Search engines read it to find pages they might otherwise miss.</p>
<h2 id="what-it-does">What it does <a class="heading-link" href="#what-it-does" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<ul>
<li>It helps new pages get found sooner, especially on a young site with few links pointing in.</li>
<li>It tells search engines when a page last changed, so they know what to fetch again.</li>
<li>It points to each language version of a page, which helps the right reader land on the right version.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="what-it-does-not-do">What it does not do <a class="heading-link" href="#what-it-does-not-do" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>A sitemap does not make a page rank. A weak page listed in a sitemap is still a weak page. It also does not replace links: a page that only appears in the sitemap, with no links from your own posts, still looks unimportant. See <a href="/blog/why-internal-links-matter/">why internal links matter</a>.</p>
<h2 id="keep-it-clean">Keep it clean <a class="heading-link" href="#keep-it-clean" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2>
<p>List only pages you want in search results. Leave out drafts, redirects and pages marked <code>noindex</code>. A sitemap full of pages that should not be there teaches search engines to trust it less.</p>

</div>
